{ dg-options "-fstropping=supper -fnest-java" }

{ hashmaps.a68 - the module Hashmaps of a68j.  Generated by
  tools/gen_hashmaps.py; do not edit.

  Hash maps (hash tables) with expected constant time for looking up,
  inserting and removing a key, one mode per key and value mode:

      keys       int, long int, string
      values     int, long int, real, long real, bool, string

  The mode of a map from K to V is K_V_map, e.g. Int_int_map,
  String_int_map, Long_int_string_map, String_long_real_map (18 modes).
  As in all bold words, the underscores do not matter: Stringintmap is
  String_int_map.

  Keys and values are kept unboxed in Java arrays (a68j.jlib.IntIntMap ...);
  string keys and values are copied.  KEYS and VALUES give the entries in
  the order in which their keys were first inserted (after a removal, a
  key is new again), so a program prints the same on every run.

  A map is a Java object: a name that refers to no Algol 68 value.  It can
  be passed around, compared with IS, ISNT and NIL and given to the
  operators and procedures below, but not dereferenced or assigned to.
  For a map m of mode M from K to V:

      new_m                    a new empty map, e.g. new_string_int_map
      new_m_with_capacity (n)  the same with room for n entries
      k AT m                   the name of the value of the key k; a missing key is
                               inserted with the value 0, 0.0, FALSE or "":
                               k AT m := v, k AT m +:= 1
      k ELEM m                 the value of the key k; a runtime error if it is missing
      m HAS k                  whether the key k is in the map
      m REMOVE k               remove the key k; yields whether it was in the map
      SIZE m                   the number of entries
      CLEAR m                  remove all entries; yields m
      KEYS m                   the keys in insertion order, a row [1 : SIZE m]
      VALUES m                 the values in the same order

  The operators work for all the modes; AT, HAS and REMOVE have priority 7.
  For string keys, AT, ELEM, HAS and REMOVE also take a char ("x" is a
  char, and an operand is not rowed to a string).
  The priorities come from the module Lists, which this module makes public
  (access pub Lists): a program that accesses Hashmaps can use the lists of
  Lists too, and must not access Lists as well (a program can access only
  one module that declares the priority of an operator, as with ga68).
  After m HAS k, the lookup of k by ELEM or AT is free (the map remembers
  the last entry found), so (m HAS k | k ELEM m | default) costs a single
  lookup.

  Example (counting words):

      access Hashmaps (
          String_int_map count = new_string_int_map;
          [] string words = ("to", "be", "or", "not", "to", "be");
          for i from LWB words to UPB words do words[i] AT count +:= 1 od;
          [] string k = KEYS count;
          for i from LWB k to UPB k do
              puts (k[i] + " " + whole (k[i] ELEM count, 0) + "'n")
          od
      )

  Bundled with a68j-backend.jar; a program needs -fnest-java only to
  declare formal holes of its own, not to access this module. }

module Hashmaps =
access pub Lists
